  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A welcome break from the episodic precip \npattern and high humidity will begin today and continue into \nthe upcoming weekend before thunderstorm chances return Sunday.\n\nThe nose of a 110 KT upper level jet will moving southeast\nacross Central PA early this Thursday morning with a quite \nstrong, diffluent patter between it, and another just segment\nacross Northern New York. \n\nThis Meso-B scale forcing has led to an expanding (but still\nquite focused) area of showers and a few low-topped TSRA that \nbrought a general 1-2 tenths of rainfall in about a 15 minute \nperiod as they moved through KUNV and surrounding areas over the\npast 1-2 hours. This convection will impact the Lower and \nMiddle Susq Valley through shortly after daybreak, with just a \nfew stray/brief showers possible during the 12-15Z window -\nmainly across the NW Mtns of PA where a BKN-OVC stratocu layer\ncould persist until around 15Z.\n\nShort range ensemble guidance indicates that mid morning clouds\nwill quickly break with a decent amount of sunshine (mixed with\ncirrus) developing by shortly after daybreak across the Central\nRidge and Valley Region of the state.\n\nThe remnants of an area of strong to potentially severe TSRA\nover the Midwest later this evening will move quickly east\novernight as a remnant MCV and likely link up with the right\nentrance region of a strong jet core stretching from Southern\nOntario to the New England Coast, bringing another round of \nclouds (and a few to several hour period of showers - mainly in\nthe late morning through mid afternoon across the SW 1/3 of the\nstate. \n\nClearing with a light northerly breeze and comfortably lower  \nhumidity will follow for Friday night and Saturday. \n\nLooks like the more active day may be Sunday vs. Monday, but\ncould see the activity linger into Monday. Like recent events,\nwarm advection and directional shear support storm formation, \nas the warm air moves back into the area on Sunday.\n\nLatest trends suggest the front will have a harder time pushing\nsouth of the MD line into the weekend. We still expect a brief \nbreak in the episodic tstm precip pattern and very muggy \nconditions Friday night into Saturday, but the chance of rain \nwill linger over portions of the southern tier of CPA. Ensemble \ndata shows a ramp in precip odds for Sunday into Sunday night in\nadvance of a frontal system pushing eastward from the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley.",
